#{[17592186247050 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\,u\\ge 0,\\,0\\le v<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(1,0,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} &=1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v &=0 \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨2(1),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨0,\\,2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,0)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,0,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-2,\\,0,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (x-1)+0\\cdot y+4\\cdot (z-1) & =0 \\\\ -2x+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ -x+2z-1 & =0 \\\\ x-2z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247051 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{3},\\,u{v}^{2},\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(2,9,3)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{3} &=2 \\\\ u{v}^{2} &=9 \\\\ v &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2uv,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨6(1)^{2},\\,{3}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,9,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨0,\\,2(1)(3),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,3)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,\\,-6,\\,36⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨9,\\,-6,\\,36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-9,\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot (x-2)+(-6)\\cdot (y-9)+36\\cdot (z-3) & =0 \\\\ 9x-6y+36z-72 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+12z & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247052 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,uv⟩,\\,u\\ge 0,\\,v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(1,4,2)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {v}^{2} &=4 \\\\ uv &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =⟨2(1),\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,2) & =⟨0,\\,2(2),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,2)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,2) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,\\,-2,\\,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-8,\\,-2,\\,8⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-4,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (x-1)+(-2)\\cdot (y-4)+8\\cdot (z-2) & =0 \\\\ -8x-2y+8z & =0 \\\\ 4x+y-4z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247053 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2},\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩,\\,u\\ge 0,\\,0\\le v<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(0,1,-2)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v &=0 \\\\ {u}^{2} &=1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2u,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0,\\,-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2(1),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\pi ) & =⟨1\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\pi )\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,\\,2,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-1,\\,z+2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot x+2\\cdot (y-1)+2\\cdot (z+2) & =0 \\\\ 2y+2z+2 & =0 \\\\ y+z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247057 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{v}^{2},\\,2v,\\,4u⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right),\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,\\,4,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247058 :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2u \\\\ 1 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,2v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247059 :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-{e}^{v}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-{e}^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{v}\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,{e}^{v},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247060 :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2}+1,\\,{v}^{3}+1,\\,2u+v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3{v}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{v}^{2},\\,-2u,\\,6u{v}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247062 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(2,-4,-3)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1+u &=2 \\\\ 2v &=-4 \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,-2) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-2) & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-4,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,-2)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-2) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,-4,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247065 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & {e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v),\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-{e}^{2u},\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-{e}^{2u})^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+({e}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}{sin}^{2}⁡v+{e}^{2u}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{4u}+{e}^{2u}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}({e}^{2u}+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{u}\\sqrt{{e}^{2u}+1}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247071 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 0 & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & 2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u{sin}^{2}⁡v-2u{cos}^{2}⁡v,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-2u,\\,2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2u)^{2}+(2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)^{2}+(4{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡v+16{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}({sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v)+12{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{4}+12{u}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}(1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v} \\\\  & =2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{u}^{2}}=u$ since $u\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2u\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}+3{u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247080 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(1,2,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=1 \\\\ 1+u &=2 \\\\ {u}^{2}{v}^{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2u{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2{u}^{2}v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2(1)(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2(1)^{2}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247082 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(2,3,4)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡v &=1 \\\\ u &=1 \\\\ (1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡v &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}((1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}((1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,1,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}((1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}((1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0,\\,-(1+{u}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,1,\\,2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨(1+(1)^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,0,\\,-(1+(1)^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,\\sqrt{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,4,\\,-\\sqrt{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247100 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-2u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,-5,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-5)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\nof radius $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{30}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247139 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(1,2,0)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u+v &=1 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} &=2 \\\\ v &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨1,\\,4(1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨1,\\,2(0),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,2,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,0)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-1,\\,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247141 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,\\,-1,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-5,\\,-1,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 1},\n$$\n\nof radius $1.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{35}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262756 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩=⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩=⟨2,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and the principal normal $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =i \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,0⟩| \\\\  & =|2+0+0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262808 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),\\sqrt{3},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+2)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+2)),0-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262836 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(6i-j+4k)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(9i+j)-(5i+5k) \\\\  & =4i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =6i+j+5k \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262845 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(4i+8j+2k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(5j+6k)-(2i+6j+6k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))j+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186262848 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(5i+j+7k)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(4i+2j)-(6i+j+5k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i-9j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262849 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =x-w \\\\  & =(i+7j+3k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(6i+12j+8k)-(3i+9j+4k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+4k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))i-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))j+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-5i+5j \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262863 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-4i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+4k)-(4i-j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)i-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-12i+6j-12k \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262864 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(3j+k)-(3i+4k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262865 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3j+4k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(-i+4j+k) \\\\  & =2i+k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-i+5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262874 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-9(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)+3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =0-27+15 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262882 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186262882 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(4-0)-1(1-0)-1(-3-20) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-23) \\\\  & =-8-1-23 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262883 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $q\\cdot (r\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $q,$ $r,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot (r\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 9-4\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 9-7\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-20+16-24 \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262892 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))-2(4\\cdot 3-(-5)\\cdot (-1))+3(4\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12-14+30 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|28|=28.\n$$"] [17592186262893 :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =14+12 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|26|=26.\n$$"] [17592186262894 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)-(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3)+4(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-3k+1+32 \\\\  & =33-3k \\\\  & =3(11-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 3(11-k) & =0 \\\\ 11-k & =0 \\\\ k & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=11.$"] [17592186262898 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t(2\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)-t(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 6)+(3\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-24t+18t+6 \\\\  & =-6t+6 \\\\  & =6(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 6(1-t) & =0 \\\\ 1-t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=1.$"] [17592186262899 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 7 \\\\ t & 4 & t \\\\ 2 & 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & t \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(4\\cdot 8-6\\cdot t)+(t\\cdot 8-2\\cdot t)+7(t\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =96-18t+8t-2t+42t-56 \\\\  & =30t+40 \\\\  & =10(3t+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 10(3t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186262906 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(9-5)-1(6-3)-1(10-9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-3-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true."] [17592186262907 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(8i+5j+9k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =i-j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(7i+4j+2k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(5i+7j+4k)-(7i+6j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-2))+(0\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+5(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2-4-20 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"] [17592186262908 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3)-3(2-1)-5(6-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2-3-25 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(T)=-30$ are true."] [17592186262912 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+7k)-(5i+j+4k) \\\\  & =-i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+j+5k)-(5i+j+4k) \\\\  & =-2i+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+3k)-(5i+j+4k) \\\\  & =-3i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)-0+3((-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2-12 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-10|=10.\n$$"] [17592186262913 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$R$ is a singular matrix” and $det(R)=0$ are true."] [17592186262917 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ y & 1\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ y & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1-y)-5\\cdot (2-2y) \\\\  & =12y-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $12,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-12 & =12 \\\\ 12y & =24 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262918 :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}q & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & q \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (q+25)-3\\cdot (-1)+5 \\\\  & =2q+58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $28,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q+58 & =28 \\\\ 2q & =-30 \\\\ q & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262963 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y+10=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y+z+15=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3i-2j-10k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262964 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262965 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+6=0\\,⟹\\,z=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨3,0,-6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y+z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3,0,-6⟩+t⟨-1,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3-6=-3,$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,2⟩.$"] [17592186262979 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$"] [17592186262986 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$"] [17592186262995 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\nx+0-2z-8=0\\,⟹\\,x=2z+8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=2z+8$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2z+8)+0-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ 4z+16-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2(0)+8=8$ and $a=⟨8,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+3y-2z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,3,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y-3z-16=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,3,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨8,0,0⟩+t⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8+0=8,$ and $b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩.$"] [17592186262998 :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"] [17592186262999 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-5y+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-5,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-5,1⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5i+5j+15k \\\\  & =⟨5,5,15⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes are parallel” is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [17592186263001 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [17592186263002 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."] [17592186263003 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$” is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not para